      "id": 1,
      "text": "San Dimas was originally called Mud Springs because of a wet marsh; renamed in 1887 by Spanish settlers to attract residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://lacountylibrary.org/sandimas-local-history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Santa Fe Railroad in 1887 turned San Dimas into a citrus hub, with the first Santa Fe depot built there.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sandimashistorical.org/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Electricity reached San Dimas in 1902, replacing kerosene lamps that had lit the town for decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://sandimashistorical.org/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "San Dimas lies about thirty miles east of downtown Los Angeles in the foothills of the San Gabriel Mountains, straddling the San Gabriel and Pomona valleys.",
